  "final_answer": "I found a match on Coursera: **3D Printing and Additive Manufacturing Specialization**, offered by the **University of Illinois Urbana-Champaign** (https://www.coursera.org/specializations/3d-printing-additive-manufacturing).\n\nIt fits all three of your criteria:\n- **Level:** Beginner level (no prior experience required)\n- **Duration:** 2 months to complete at ~10 hours a week — squarely inside your 1–3 month window\n- **Provider:** University of Illinois Urbana-Champaign, a renowned public research university, with instructors including Vishal Sachdev, Jeffrey Smith and Matthew Griffin\n\nIt's a 4-course series (~77 hours total) that takes you from the fundamentals of how 3D printing works, through 3D modeling and scanning software, to setting up, operating and maintaining a desktop 3D printer. It has 20,847 learners enrolled and a 4.6-star rating across 2,494 reviews, and it awards a shareable career certificate from the university. The courses are: The 3D Printing Revolution (10 hrs), 3D Printing Applications (21 hrs), 3D Printing Software (15 hrs), and 3D Printing Hardware (31 hrs).\n\nOne alternative I checked and ruled out: Arizona State University's \"Rapid Prototyping Using 3D Printing\" is also beginner-level and university-provided, but it's listed at only 4 weeks to complete, which falls short of your 1-month minimum.",
